    "file": "backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 128,
    "summary": "`env_allowlists_naming` reads all four allowlist settings unconditionally, whereas `membership_service._access_allowlists` (the predicate every door actually enforces) deliberately ignores an unconfigured auth mode (OIDC list only when `generic_oidc_configured and oidc_allowlist_configured`; OTP list only when `otp_auth_configured`). The new helper therefore classifies as ENV_LISTED addresses the doors do not admit via env at all, and step (d) skips membership removal on that basis.",
    "failure_scenario": "Deployment is generic-OIDC with membership enforced via the table; a stale `AUTH_OTP_ALLOWED_EMAILS=alice@acme.io` is still in the env from a previous OTP setup (OTP mailer block unset, so `otp_auth_configured` is False and `_decide` never consults it). Admin offboards Alice: preview says `env_listed` naming AUTH_OTP_ALLOWED_EMAILS, the pass revokes her PAT/sessions but leaves her `workspace_members` row and reports 'remove it there instead'. The operator removes the (inert) OTP entry; Alice signs in through OIDC again — her row still admits her, and nothing in the receipt or the Members page says the offboarding was incomplete."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 204,
    "summary": "`_membership` returns ENV_LISTED (and skips removal) even when a real `workspace_members` row exists, diverging from `remove_member`, whose `_refuse_env_removal` only fires when there is NO row (a synthetic env-row id). The standalone Members page would delete the row; the composed pass refuses to, so after the env entry is removed the table row still grants membership.",
    "failure_scenario": "Bob is both in `OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS` and has an admin-invited member row. Offboarding reports `remove_membership` skipped with 'remove it there instead'. The operator edits the env var and restarts, believing the receipt's instruction closed access. Bob's member row remains (the pass never deleted it and the receipt's `membership_id` is non-null only as an opaque hint), so his next OIDC sign-in is admitted by the table under `union(env, table)`. The advertised 'remove it there instead' is not sufficient, and the one place that could have deleted the row was told not to."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 383,
    "summary": "Sessions are revoked by a direct `row.revoked_at = now` write, bypassing `session_service.revoke`, which emits the `session_revoked` structured log line (`session_id`, `user_id`). No per-session log or audit event is produced here; only a count lands in the receipt/summary log and the `user.offboard` audit `after` payload. PAT revocation likewise skips `revoke_key`'s per-key `api_key_revoked` log (though it does write the audit event).",
    "failure_scenario": "A departed user's session appears in a later incident timeline (e.g. a request logged with `mode=session session_id=X` shortly before the offboarding). Operators search logs for `session_revoked session_id=X` — the marker every other revocation path emits — and find nothing, concluding the session was never revoked and escalating, when in fact it was revoked in this pass. ADR 0041 defers session-lifecycle audit, so the log line is the only revocation trail for sessions and this path drops it."
  },
